narrative:
Old veteran Mara Dain warms her hands over a brazier and laughs without humor.

@Mara: Third war over the same fields in my life. Every treaty drew a line. None of them asked who had been buried on either side of it.

She forgets to thank you for listening, then remembers an insult from thirty years ago with perfect clarity.

Recruitment drums answer each other across the market. The notices promise wages, honor, and a swift campaign. Ser Aldous Fenn is pinning one back up after someone tears it down.

@Aldous: I am being sent north. I would rather you heard it from me than from a list.

He presses the paper flat with a careful hand.

At the border council, maps cover a table too small for the men leaning over it. Warlord Threska Cael waits outside the tent under a truce flag. Lieutenant Casswell Marrow calls the meeting a waste of daylight.

@Threska: I want the farms your king took. I do not want boys frozen in them.
@Casswell: Hear that? Even their threats are farming talk.

The War-Crow lands on a snapped lance before dawn. By noon, the forward line has folded around a farmhouse and Ser Aldous is pinned there with the wounded. Orin Vasse is carrying bandages with both hands red.

@Orin: I can save whoever reaches me. I cannot reach everyone.

Across the field, Threska's banner hesitates instead of advancing.

Threska walks into the mud alone. Aldous, pale beneath the blood on his sleeve, meets her halfway. Neither side receives what it came to take. Both sides receive the chance to count their dead before making more.

@Threska: Put the farms under shared law. Let the graves belong to no crown.
@Aldous: It is not victory.
@Threska: Then perhaps it can last.
